---
description: Reactive UI pattern for filters / sorting / selections
globs: *.dart
alwaysApply: false
---
Reactive UI pattern for filters / sorting / selections:

  Goal: Immediate visual feedback, no null crashes, and proper data refresh.

  1. Immediate UI updates (local state)
     - Use StatefulBuilder when the selection is inside a dialog/bottom sheet/popup:
       StatefulBuilder(
         builder: (context, setLocalState) {
           return SelectionWidget(
             value: _selectedValue,
             onSelected: (option) {
               if (option == null) return;

               setState(() {
                 _selectedValue = option;
               });
               setLocalState(() {}); // force local rebuild if needed

               _fetchData(); // apply changes
             },
           );
         },
       );

  2. Null-safe handling
     - Always check for null in callbacks:
       onSelected: (option) {
         if (option == null) return;
         // update state + refresh
       };

  3. Mandatory selections
     - If selection cannot be null:
       - Disable "All"/null options (e.g. showAllOption: false).
       - Set and keep a sensible default.
       - Reflect this in widget parameters / docs.

  4. Trigger data updates after selection
     - After updating state, immediately refresh data/UI:
       onSelected: (option) {
         if (option == null) return;
         setState(() { _selected = option; });
         _fetchData(); // or notify provider, etc.
       };

  5. General rules
     - Use local state (StatefulBuilder) for instant visual feedback where needed.
     - Keep filter/sort state in a clear source of truth (page state or provider).
     - Avoid "null check operator used on a null value" by always guarding inputs.

